    "created_at": "2026-02-11T08:04:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-02-11T08:04:52Z",
    "body": "> However, should we add a note in the HTML spec to explicitly clarify the difference between the `:stalled` pseudo-class and the `stalled` event? Since they share the same name and apply to the same element, it would be helpful to clearly document their distinction to avoid potential confusion.\n\nThat's a good idea. If you'd like to send a PR, I can review.\n\n> I believe several WPTs need updates:\n> \n> * [media-loading-state.html](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/2d32e15251d41e4e97e3c5be9217f03be248dbbf/css/selectors/media/media-loading-state.html) and\n>   [media-loading-pseudo-classes-in-has.html](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/bd8b39ea5b522a67696972551690cc36a9f05621/css/selectors/invalidation/media-loading-pseudo-classes-in-has.html#L4)\n>   should use a more robust way to trigger the `stalled` event on a media element. The current test implementation does not work in Firefox.\n\nYeah, the `pipe=trickle` approach didn't work in Chromium either. I think the stall-resume.py I added should work for these tests too, if you'd like to try fixing them.\n\n> * [media-loading-state-timing.sub.html](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/2d32e15251/css/selectors/media/media-loading-state-timing.sub.html)\n>   needs to update its assertion for matching `:stalled` only when the element’s ready state is [HAVE_CURRENT_DATA](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#dom-media-have_current_data) or less.\n\nYep. The test needs to play until it reaches the end of the buffered range. To ensure `readyState` is used, maybe it should check if `:buffering` and `:stalled` match in each \"timeupdate\" event where `readyState` is still `HAVE_FUTURE_DATA` or higher.\n\nHappy to review test changes for this.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
